editorial:
## Method and reasoning
While searching for the minimum, compare the midpoint with the right endpoint. If the midpoint is larger, the pivot lies strictly to its right. Otherwise the pivot is at or left of the midpoint. The virtual array a[(pivot+i)%n] is increasing, so search it and translate the index back.

## Walk through the sample
The minimum −2 is at physical position 4. Virtual sorted positions therefore read −2, 1, 4, 7, 10, 15. A hit on 10 maps back to physical position 2.

## Complexity
Time O((q+1) log n) after reading, space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (auto &x : a)
        cin >> x;
    int l = 0, r = n - 1;
    while (l < r) {
        int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
        if (a[m] > a[r])
            l = m + 1;
        else
            r = m;
    }
    int pivot = l;
    while (q--) {
        int x;
        cin >> x;
        l = 0;
        r = n;
        while (l < r) {
            int m = l + (r - l) / 2;
            if (a[(pivot + m) % n] < x)
                l = m + 1;
            else
                r = m;
        }
        int at = (pivot + l) % n;
        cout << (l < n && a[at] == x ? at + 1 : 0) << '\n';
    }
}
~~~
